1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,901
The internet ran out of addresses in 2011.

2
00:00:02,901 --> 00:00:05,571
Every device online needs an IP address.

3
00:00:05,571 --> 00:00:09,713
The original system, IPv4, uses 32 bits.

4
00:00:09,713 --> 00:00:12,194
That's about 4.3 billion addresses.

5
00:00:12,194 --> 00:00:15,302
Sounds like a lot, until there are more phones than people.

6
00:00:15,302 --> 00:00:18,938
In 2011, the central pool handed out its last blocks.

7
00:00:18,938 --> 00:00:21,537
So your whole home shares one public address.

8
00:00:21,537 --> 00:00:23,889
Your router keeps track of who asked for what.

9
00:00:23,889 --> 00:00:28,074
The real fix is IPv6, with 128 bits.

10
00:00:28,074 --> 00:00:30,994
Enough for every grain of sand on Earth, many times over.
